WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.920
Let's get into first primaries creating daily episode 16.

00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:07.100
It's June 21, 2026.

00:00:07.940 --> 00:00:11.900
Today's question, what would this cost if you only paid for the atoms?

00:00:12.780 --> 00:00:20.460
An LED bulb that cost $50 in 2010 now sells for a few dollars because its price was driven

00:00:20.460 --> 00:00:24.400
toward the cost of the semiconductor dye and phosphor inside.

00:00:24.400 --> 00:00:32.000
In 2010, a typical LED replacement for a 60 watt incandescent carried a retail price between

00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:33.280
$40 and $50.

00:00:34.640 --> 00:00:37.940
The same light output today is available for two to three dollars.

00:00:38.580 --> 00:00:43.180
The difference did not come from cheaper electricity or government rebates alone.

00:00:44.020 --> 00:00:49.100
It came from repeated attacks on the physical bill of materials and the manufacturing steps

00:00:49.100 --> 00:00:51.640
that turned those materials into a finished lamp.

00:00:52.640 --> 00:00:56.920
Lighting manufacturers had spent a century refining the incandescent lab.

00:00:57.340 --> 00:01:02.480
The design started with a glass envelope, a tungsten filament and a simple screw base.

00:01:03.300 --> 00:01:08.740
Every incremental improvement kept those elements in place and focused on longer life or slightly

00:01:08.740 --> 00:01:09.720
better efficacy.

00:01:10.460 --> 00:01:15.120
When white LEDs first reached commercial volumes, the same habit persisted.

00:01:15.800 --> 00:01:19.220
Early LED bulbs were assembled like specialty electronics.

00:01:19.940 --> 00:01:24.920
A small number of high brightness dies were purchased at low yield from wafer fabs.

00:01:25.320 --> 00:01:29.040
Those dies were mounted on metal core printed circuit boards.

00:01:29.740 --> 00:01:32.280
They were covered with hand mixed phosphor.

00:01:32.960 --> 00:01:38.280
Then they were bolted to extruded aluminum heat sinks, sized for worst case thermal loads.

00:01:39.000 --> 00:01:44.580
Driver circuits were built from discrete components on separate boards because that was how power

00:01:44.580 --> 00:01:46.300
supplies had always been made.

00:01:47.100 --> 00:01:52.880
The result was a parts count in the dozens, multiple separate suppliers and a finished cost

00:01:52.880 --> 00:01:57.960
that reflected low volume assembly rather than the underlying physics of light emission.

00:01:58.600 --> 00:02:03.840
Because the industry had never started from the question, what does it actually take to produce

00:02:03.840 --> 00:02:05.160
photons from electrons?

00:02:06.020 --> 00:02:10.340
The price remained anchored to the cost of the previous generation's assembly methods.

00:02:11.220 --> 00:02:17.200
Engineers instead asked what atoms are required to generate white light at high efficiency.

00:02:18.020 --> 00:02:22.340
A rough magic wand estimate begins with the semiconductor die itself.

00:02:23.060 --> 00:02:29.440
That die is a few square millimeters of gallium nitride material grown on sapphire or silicon

00:02:29.440 --> 00:02:30.240
carbide.

00:02:30.820 --> 00:02:37.080
At commodity wafer prices, the gallium, indium, and nitrogen content of one die is measured

00:02:37.080 --> 00:02:38.360
in fractions of a cent.

00:02:39.240 --> 00:02:45.120
The phosphor layer that converts blue emission to broad spectrum white uses a few milligrams

00:02:45.120 --> 00:02:48.360
of yttrium aluminum garnet doped with serium.

00:02:49.540 --> 00:02:53.800
Commodity rare earth prices place that quantity in the low sense.

00:02:54.420 --> 00:02:59.620
The aluminum heat sink once the die and phosphor are efficient enough to produce little waste heat

00:02:59.620 --> 00:03:02.860
can be reduced to a few grams of stamped sheet.

00:03:03.580 --> 00:03:05.880
Its material value is again pennies.

00:03:06.600 --> 00:03:12.800
The driver electronic shrink to a small integrated circuit and a handful of passive components whose

00:03:12.800 --> 00:03:16.120
silicon and copper content is likewise negligible.

00:03:16.860 --> 00:03:22.080
Adding these figures produces a raw material floor on the order of tens of cents per lamp,

00:03:22.500 --> 00:03:23.140
not dollars.

00:03:23.920 --> 00:03:29.880
Early commercial LED bulbs therefore carried an idiot index of roughly 50 to 100.

00:03:30.680 --> 00:03:35.260
Consider a $50 product whose materials were worth perhaps 50 cents.

00:03:35.920 --> 00:03:40.640
Divide the finished price by the material cost and the ratio lands near 100.

00:03:41.300 --> 00:03:47.680
That ratio signals the design and process steps, not the atoms were carrying almost all the

00:03:47.680 --> 00:03:48.100
cost.

00:03:48.900 --> 00:03:52.300
The first decisive move was to increase dies per wafer.

00:03:53.020 --> 00:03:58.880
Larger waifers and higher yields spread the fixed cost of epicycle growth across thousands

00:03:58.880 --> 00:04:00.540
of devices instead of dozens.

00:04:01.680 --> 00:04:03.840
The second move attacked the force force step.

00:04:04.480 --> 00:04:10.160
Instead of mixing and dispensing force force slurry in small batches, manufacturers deposited

00:04:10.160 --> 00:04:16.680
uniform force for films or used remote force for architectures that allowed one film to serve

00:04:16.680 --> 00:04:17.440
many dies.

00:04:18.220 --> 00:04:20.600
Both changes removed labor and variation.

00:04:20.600 --> 00:04:27.600
The third move was thermal, as efficacy rose from roughly 60 lumens per watt to more

00:04:27.600 --> 00:04:31.340
than 100, the waste heat per lumens fell sharply.

00:04:32.040 --> 00:04:37.220
Heat sink mass could therefore be cut by half or more without raising junction temperature.

00:04:37.940 --> 00:04:42.340
The remaining aluminum could be stamped or cast rather than machined.

00:04:42.980 --> 00:04:45.200
The fourth move integrated the driver.

00:04:46.200 --> 00:04:50.420
Custom applications specific integrated circuits replaced discrete boards.

00:04:50.880 --> 00:04:56.060
This eliminated connectors multiple assembly stations and associated failure points.

00:04:56.820 --> 00:05:01.540
Each of these steps was possible only after the requirement itself had been questioned.

00:05:02.180 --> 00:05:06.180
The original heat sink size had been sized for an inefficient die.

00:05:07.100 --> 00:05:09.780
Once the die improved, the requirement changed.

00:05:10.440 --> 00:05:12.440
The same logic applied to the driver.

00:05:12.440 --> 00:05:19.700
Once the LED string voltage and current requirement stabilized, a single low cost IC could

00:05:19.700 --> 00:05:22.220
replace an entire discrete power supply.

00:05:22.900 --> 00:05:27.540
Retail prices fell more than 90% while efficacy roughly doubled.

00:05:28.140 --> 00:05:34.640
The idiot index of today's commodity LED bulb sits far lower than the 2010 version.

00:05:35.160 --> 00:05:40.940
It remains well above one because packaging, certification and distribution still add cost.

00:05:41.940 --> 00:05:44.560
Quality dispersion appeared as prices dropped.

00:05:45.080 --> 00:05:50.940
Some low cost lamps exhibit visible flicker or color rendering indices below 80 because

00:05:50.940 --> 00:05:54.520
corners were cut in driver design or phosphor consistency.

00:05:55.180 --> 00:05:57.640
Those trade-offs are real and measurable.

00:05:58.180 --> 00:06:03.240
They illustrate that movement toward the material floor is not automatic and can overshoot

00:06:03.240 --> 00:06:05.740
if critical performance attributes are ignored.

00:06:05.740 --> 00:06:11.340
A product whose price greatly exceeds the value of its raw atoms is announcing a design

00:06:11.340 --> 00:06:14.280
or process problem, not a material shortage.

00:06:15.080 --> 00:06:20.420
The gap between the finish price and the magic wand number points directly at assembly steps,

00:06:20.900 --> 00:06:26.320
part counts, and inherited requirements rather than any scarcity of gallium or aluminum.

00:06:27.140 --> 00:06:32.800
When teams treat that gap as a signal instead of an inevitability, they begin to ask different

00:06:32.800 --> 00:06:34.660
questions about each component.

00:06:34.660 --> 00:06:40.360
They ask whether the heat sink must still weigh what it did when the dye produced twice the

00:06:40.360 --> 00:06:40.900
waste heat.

00:06:41.460 --> 00:06:46.600
They ask whether the driver must still be built from discrete parts once voltage and current

00:06:46.600 --> 00:06:49.620
needs settle into a narrow, repeatable range.

00:06:50.240 --> 00:06:53.260
Each time the requirement is re-examined first.

00:06:53.940 --> 00:06:58.920
The part that satisfies it can be simplified or integrated without sacrificing the performance

00:06:58.920 --> 00:07:00.780
that actually matters to the user.

00:07:00.780 --> 00:07:04.820
That sequence repeated across the LED supply chain.

00:07:05.740 --> 00:07:11.140
Larger waifers became viable only after yields improved enough to justify the capital.

00:07:11.980 --> 00:07:16.740
Film phosphor became viable only after the dye's output spectrum stabilized.

00:07:17.560 --> 00:07:22.800
Stamped heat sinks became viable only after efficacy gains reduced the thermal load.

00:07:23.520 --> 00:07:28.800
The integrated driver became viable only after the rest of the system settled into predictable

00:07:28.800 --> 00:07:29.900
electrical boundaries.

00:07:30.800 --> 00:07:36.240
In each case the cost reduction followed the redefinition of what the part had to do, not

00:07:36.240 --> 00:07:39.280
an optimization of the old part in its old role.

00:07:40.080 --> 00:07:45.280
The same pattern now appears in other solid state devices whose learning curves are still

00:07:45.280 --> 00:07:45.620
young.

00:07:46.380 --> 00:07:52.520
Power electronics, battery management systems, and certain sensor arrays still carry idiot

00:07:52.520 --> 00:07:54.400
indices well above one.

00:07:55.400 --> 00:08:00.340
Their prices reflect assembly methods and thermal margins sized for earlier generations

00:08:00.340 --> 00:08:01.360
of the technology.

00:08:01.840 --> 00:08:06.760
The open question is which industry will next treat its current price tag as a temporary

00:08:06.760 --> 00:08:10.140
engineering artifact rather than an immutable fact.

00:08:10.860 --> 00:08:15.380
Watch for the first company that publishes the material cost breakdown of its flagship

00:08:15.380 --> 00:08:21.260
product and then begins cutting the gap between that number and the selling price by redesigning

00:08:21.260 --> 00:08:22.660
the requirement itself.

00:08:23.360 --> 00:08:26.080
That's a wrap on today's first principles breakdown.

00:08:26.720 --> 00:08:29.280
The idea is travel further when you pass them on.

00:08:29.920 --> 00:08:31.460
Send this to a fellow builder.

00:08:32.080 --> 00:08:34.340
I'm Patrick in Vancouver, see you tomorrow.

